charseg_model_dialogue.tsv — provenance and licensing
=====================================================

What it is
----------
A character-level sentence-boundary classifier (averaged perceptron over hashed
character n-gram + character-type features) specialised for dialogue- and quote-
heavy prose (conversational text, quoted speech). It is the fast char-level
counterpart to the word-level CRF (sentence/crf): no word tokenizer, a single
pass over characters, and a tiny hashed weight table.

Exposed as the opt-in charseg.Dialogue() specialist. For general multi-domain
text use the recommended default, charseg.Default() (the permissive clean model
in charseg_model_clean.tsv); this dialogue model transfers poorly to formal prose.

How it was made (recipe)
------------------------
Weak supervision (self-labelled silver corpus), no external gold corpus — the
same labelling as the CRF dialogue model, so the two are directly comparable:

  1. Source text: text we own (used with permission). No third-party corpus is
     used.
  2. Silver labelling: tokenize a line with the default segmenter to find silver
     sentence boundaries from ORTHOGRAPHIC signals only — a token ending in a
     closing quote (” " » 」 』 ’) or terminal punctuation (. ! ? … 。 ！ ？), and
     the token before an opening dialogue quote (“ « 「 『 „). Bare inter-word
     spaces are NON-boundaries. The silver boundary is then projected onto the
     last CHARACTER of the ending token; the classifier is trained per character.
  3. Train: averaged perceptron (charseg.Train), 12 iterations, feature hashing
     into 2^20 float32 weights (stored sparse; ~3k nonzero → ~21 KB).

Measured quality (held-out silver; see cmd/charsegtrain)
--------------------------------------------------------
  - Internal doc-disjoint held-out (no-leak model): boundary-F1 0.9950
      (P 0.9995 / R 0.9905), vs CRF-dialogue 0.9895, whitespace 0.3240.
  - Fully separate held-out FILE: boundary-F1 0.7733 (P 1.0000 / R 0.6304),
      tying the CRF-dialogue model.
  - UD_Thai-PUD (out-of-domain, never trained on): 0.0792 — like CRF-dialogue
      (0.0799); both dialogue-trained models transfer poorly to formal PUD prose.

Speed (see BenchmarkEngines): ~25x faster than the CRF and needs no tokenizer.

Licensing
---------
Derived from text we own via self weak-supervision. The model stores aggregate
hashed feature weights (statistical parameters, not reconstructable prose).
Shippable under the project licence.
